<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores facts in tables and lets an application ask questions about them, change related facts together, and reject changes that break declared rules. A shop can keep its customers, products and orders there. When Mina buys the last mug, the database can record her order and reduce the stock as one decision, even while another customer is trying to buy it.</p>
<p>That combination is useful wherever several parts of an application share facts that must agree: bookings and capacity, accounts and transfers, subscriptions and entitlements. Keeping the facts in files is possible too, but the application then has to build its own machinery for finding records, coordinating writers and recovering incomplete changes after a crash.</p>
<p>The tables are only the beginning. A query that returns four rows may read millions to find them. An order can commit even though the customer sees an error. A second server may have a copy that is already out of date. Understanding those boundaries is how you turn a database into a reliable application.</p>
<p>Every example here follows an invented shop selling tea and tea things. Mina Shah buys one blue mug and two packets of green tea in order 103. We will first read the records that describe that purchase, then follow what happens when it competes with another buyer, commits, and needs to survive a failure. PostgreSQL 18 supplies the concrete SQL and concurrency behaviour; the final sections compare the arrangements you might choose.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="facts"><h2 id="facts">Tables describe different kinds of fact</h2>
<p>A <strong>table</strong> is a named collection of rows. Each <strong>row</strong> describes one thing of the kind that table records. Each named <strong>column</strong> describes one attribute, with a type that determines which values it can hold. In <code>products</code>, one row describes one product, <code>name</code> holds text, and <code>stock</code> holds an integer count.</p>
<p>Figure 1 shows the shop after Mina’s purchase has been accepted as order 103. Read across the outlined row for the mug, then down the stock column to compare it with the other products.</p>
<ProductTable />
<p>The mug’s row brings three facts together: its identifier is P7, its name is Blue mug, and its stock is 0. Each row puts the same kinds of fact in the same columns. The stock column contains 10, 0 and 4, each a count for the product on that row.</p>
<p>Customers and orders describe different things, so they have their own tables. An order keeps the customer’s identifier, <code>customer_id</code>, to say who placed it. Each purchase can contain several products, so <code>order_items</code> holds a separate row for each numbered <strong>order line</strong>, with its product, quantity and agreed price.</p>
<p>Figure 2 follows those identifiers from Mina to the mug she bought. First, her customer number 1 matches the 1 on both her orders. Then order number 103 matches its two lines. Finally, P7 on line 1 matches the mug’s product row. Each step matches values in a named column; it does not depend on where a row sits in the table.</p>
<ShopFacts />
<p>The number 1 has different meanings on that path. <code>customer_id = 1</code> identifies Mina. <code>line_no = 1</code> identifies the first line <em>within an order</em>. The column name and the row’s meaning tell you which one you are reading.</p>
<p>A <strong>primary key</strong> identifies a row uniquely and cannot be missing. A customer’s key is <code>customer_id</code>. An order line needs two columns together, <code>(order_id, line_no)</code>, because both order 101 and order 103 have a line 1. Names make poor identifiers: two customers can share a name, and a customer can change theirs.</p>
<p>A <strong>foreign key</strong> requires a value to identify an existing row in another table. The foreign key on <code>orders.customer_id</code> prevents an order referring to customer 99 when no such customer exists. It does not require every customer to have an order: the shop can register Edda without her buying anything. Nor does it mean “only one order each”. Mina’s two orders both refer to her one customer row.</p>
<p>The table definitions, types, keys and other rules together form the <strong>schema</strong>. Some rules are simple <strong>constraints</strong>: quantity must be greater than zero, stock cannot be negative, and an order’s status must be one of the allowed values. The database checks these wherever a write comes from, including a maintenance script that bypasses the shop’s web form. Choosing the rules still belongs to the people designing the application.</p>
<p>The two prices in Figure 2 deserve their separate columns. <code>products.price</code> is what the shop currently offers. <code>order_items.unit_price</code> is what was agreed for that line. Both say 20.00 for the mug today. If the offered price later becomes 22.00, Mina’s line stays at 20.00. Reading today’s offer to reconstruct yesterday’s bill would change the agreement.</p>
<p>Keeping Mina once, rather than copying her name into every order, avoids having to repair many copies when her name changes. Keeping an agreed price on every order line preserves a different fact. Good modelling starts by saying what each row and value mean; eliminating every repeated value would lose information. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relations, keys and normalisation</a> develops that judgement, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/data-lifecycle/">History, correction and deletion</a> follows facts that must survive later changes.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="questions"><h2 id="questions">SQL asks for a result</h2>
<p><strong>SQL</strong> is the language used to define these tables, read them and change their rows. A read statement is a <strong>query</strong>. It describes the answer you want; the database chooses how to find it. You can ask a new question without first arranging the stored rows in the order that question needs.</p>
<p>Suppose Mina opens her order history. We want one result row for each of her order lines, with the product’s name and the price agreed on the line. The following query asks for that result.</p>
<div class="intro-code"><Sql code={historyQuery} label="Mina’s order lines, with product names and agreed prices" /></div>
<p><code>FROM orders AS o</code> names the starting table and gives it the short name <code>o</code>. Each <code>JOIN</code> combines it with matching rows from another table; <code>ON</code> says what must match. <code>WHERE</code> keeps Mina’s orders. <code>SELECT</code> names the columns to return. Finally, <code>ORDER BY</code> puts the answer in order by order number and then line number. Without it, the order of returned rows is not promised.</p>
<div class="table-scroll intro-table" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Result of Mina’s order history query"><table><caption>Mina’s history · one row per order line</caption><thead><tr><th>order_id</th><th>status</th><th>line_no</th><th>name</th><th>quantity</th><th>unit_price</th></tr></thead><tbody>{minaLines.map(([order,line,product,quantity,price])=><tr><td>{order}</td><td>{order===101?'paid':'pending'}</td><td>{line}</td><td>{products.find(([id])=>id===product)?.[1]}</td><td>{quantity}</td><td>{price}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>Two orders produced four result rows. Joining an order to its lines repeats the order’s columns once for every matching line. The result has changed what one row represents. If you count these rows as orders, Mina appears to have four. If you sum the line values, however, the repeated order number is exactly what lets you collect the right lines: order 103 is <code>1 × 20.00 + 2 × 4.50 = 29.00</code>.</p>
<p>A <strong>grouped query</strong> can collect those lines by order number and use <code>SUM(quantity * unit_price)</code> to produce one total per order. Adding payments needs another decision about row meaning. Order 101 has two lines and two payments; joining both directly to its order makes four line–payment pairs. Adding the repeated amounts then inflates the totals. Calculating the line total and payment total separately before combining them avoids that multiplication.</p>
<p>A query also chooses what to leave out. An ordinary join to <code>payments</code> would omit order 103 because it has no payment. A left join can keep that order and supply <code>NULL</code> for the absent payment’s columns. <strong>NULL</strong> marks a missing or unknown value; it is not the number zero. A report of what customers still owe must decide how to treat that absence.</p>
<p>These are questions about the meaning of an answer before they are questions about speed. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Joins and the meaning of a result row</a> works through the multiplication and absence cases. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/aggregates-and-windows/">Aggregates, windows and ordered results</a> builds totals and calculations over groups, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/values/">Values, missing data and equality</a> explains how types and missing values affect comparisons.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="purchase"><h2 id="purchase">A purchase changes several facts together</h2>
<p>Reading a history leaves the stored facts alone. Now rewind to just before order 103. It and its lines do not yet exist, the mug’s stock is 1, and the tea’s is 12. Accepting the purchase will change those facts together. The shop must reduce the mug’s stock from 1 to 0, reduce the tea’s from 12 to 10, add the order, and add its two lines. Saving the order but forgetting to reduce stock would let the shop sell the mug again. Reducing stock but losing the order would leave a mug nobody can account for.</p>
<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups these changes into one all-or-nothing operation. The application starts it with <code>BEGIN</code>, makes the changes, and asks for <code>COMMIT</code> when all have succeeded. A <code>ROLLBACK</code> abandons them. Other transactions do not see a partly committed purchase. If the purchase aborts, its table changes do not take effect.</p>
<p>This all-or-nothing property is called <strong>atomicity</strong>. It solves a failure between the order insert and the stock update. It does not, by itself, decide what to do when two buyers both read “one mug left”.</p>
<p>At that same starting point, Mina and Tariq each want the one P7. In Figure 3, play <em>Read, calculate, write 0</em> first. Both requests read 1, subtract one in the application, and later write the literal result 0. Watch the committed orders, not just the stock count.</p>
<RaceFigure />
<p>Both orders committed, but stock never went below zero. The stock constraint accepts zero twice. Even putting each request’s steps inside a transaction permits this outcome at PostgreSQL’s default <strong>Read Committed</strong> isolation level. The second write replaces zero with zero, based on a decision that was made before the first purchase committed.</p>
<p><strong>Isolation</strong> describes how concurrent transactions may observe and affect one another. At Read Committed, an ordinary PostgreSQL <code>SELECT</code> reads a <strong>snapshot</strong> of the facts committed when that statement began, plus its own transaction’s earlier changes. It does not reserve the rows it reads, and another transaction may change them afterwards. A transaction boundary supplies a place to protect the decision; the application still needs a protocol that uses the engine’s isolation rules correctly.</p>
<p>For this stock row, combine the test and the change in one statement. Subtract one only while at least one remains, and ask the database to return the changed stock.</p>
<div class="intro-code"><Sql code={reserveQuery} label="Take one mug only if one remains; PostgreSQL RETURNING reports the changed row" /></div>
<p>Now switch Figure 3 to <em>Conditional UPDATE</em>. Mina changes the row first and holds its write <strong>lock</strong> until her transaction ends. A lock makes a conflicting operation wait. Tariq’s update waits for her, then PostgreSQL rechecks its condition against the changed row after she commits. Zero no longer meets <code>stock &gt;= 1</code>, so his statement returns no row.</p>
<p>The application must use that result. One returned row means it took the mug; no row means it must abandon this purchase. Mina uses the same approach to take two packets of tea. Only when both changes succeed does she commit the order and its lines in that transaction. If either fails, she rolls back the whole purchase, returning any stock it had taken. Every stock-decreasing writer must follow the agreed rule.</p>
<p>This protocol protects a count in one existing product row. A rule such as “the total across these warehouses must remain above ten” needs reasoning about several rows. Locks, stronger isolation and retries then have different costs. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/transaction-boundaries/">Transactions and isolation</a> develops those cases, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/locks-and-deadlocks/">Locks, contention and deadlocks</a> explains waiting and what happens when two transactions each hold something the other needs.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="work"><h2 id="work">The engine chooses work behind the statement</h2>
<p>The history query said which rows Mina wanted, without saying “read this file, then look at that address”. The database’s <strong>planner</strong> chooses an execution plan: the scans, lookups, joins and calculations that produce the answer. It estimates how much data each step will encounter and compares possible routes.</p>
<p>Without a useful access path, finding Mina’s orders may mean scanning the whole orders table and checking every row. An <strong>index</strong> stores selected values with a way to find their rows. An index beginning with <code>customer_id</code> lets the engine find Mina’s entries together and follow them to her orders. It is like a book’s index: a small ordered structure directs you to the relevant pages.</p>
<p>An index is useful when it saves enough work. A report that needs most orders may be cheaper to answer by reading the table in bulk. Each extra index also takes space and has to be maintained as data changes. Adding an index to every column exchanges read costs for write costs without establishing that any important query benefits.</p>
<p>Storage and memory shape that cost. PostgreSQL stores rows and indexes in blocks called <strong>pages</strong>, normally 8 kB each. It caches pages in memory and works on those copies. A page already in memory avoids a file read; a cache miss needs one. Sorting a large result or building a join can also need more memory than is available for that operation and spill temporary data to storage.</p>
<p>Doing less work and waiting less are different improvements. The conditional stock update may find P7 quickly through its primary key and then spend almost all its time waiting for another buyer’s lock. Another index will not release that lock. A request may instead be waiting for a free connection before its SQL reaches the server at all.</p>
<p>Begin an investigation with the slow request and a representative query, values and data volume. A query plan shows the chosen work. PostgreSQL’s <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> executes the statement and compares estimates with actual rows and times; a measured plan for a data-changing statement also changes data. Separate those execution measurements from lock waits, connection queues and the time spent sending a large answer. Then check any improvement under the concurrency the application actually expects.</p>
<p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes and access paths</a> follows values to rows and counts the saving. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">Query execution and bad plans</a> follows the work through a complete plan. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/connections-and-capacity/">Connections, overload and capacity</a> explains why allowing more simultaneous requests can make each one slower.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="commit"><h2 id="commit">What successful COMMIT establishes</h2>
<p>Mina’s transaction has made the right changes. They are still in memory, which a process crash or power cut can erase. <strong>Durability</strong> is the promise that the committed purchase can survive that crash. The database needs a recoverable record on persistent storage before it reports success.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL uses a <strong>write-ahead log</strong>, or WAL: an ordered record describing changes, kept separately from the table and index files. With normal durable settings it flushes the transaction’s log records through its commit record before replying. The changed pages can reach their files later, because recovery can repeat the logged changes if a crash interrupts that work.</p>
<p>Figure 4 follows that order. Look at where the durable record exists when the application receives the reply; it need not yet exist in the table’s page files.</p>
<CommitFigure />
<p>A restart reads the recoverable history and restores a valid committed state. Old row versions and transaction status also let PostgreSQL hide changes from transactions that never committed. The page cache warms again as requests resume, so a correctly recovered database can initially be slower.</p>
<p>The guarantee depends on the configured commit contract and on storage keeping what it says it flushed. PostgreSQL can be configured to acknowledge a commit before its log is durable, trading a shorter wait for possible loss of recent acknowledged changes. A local durable commit also does not promise survival after losing that storage altogether. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/crash-recovery/">Write-ahead logs and crash recovery</a> explains the ordering and its limits; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Row versions, storage and cleanup</a> explains why old data can continue to occupy space.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="request"><h2 id="request">A committed order and a successful request are different</h2>
<p>Suppose the database commits order 103, then the connection breaks before the application receives the success reply. Mina sees a timeout. That observation fits two histories: the transaction committed and its reply was lost, or it never committed. A timeout alone does not tell the application which happened.</p>
<p>Starting the purchase again under a fresh identity can take stock twice. Instead, give the purchase a stable request identity before the first attempt, store it with the committed result under a uniqueness rule, and reuse it when resolving uncertainty. The retry checks whether that same purchase already exists and returns its result. It must also reject reuse of the identity with different purchase details. An order number can serve only if the application preserves and reuses it across attempts; generating a new number each time defeats the purpose.</p>
<p>A confirmed transaction abort is a different case. The application knows its changes did not commit and can retry the whole decision where appropriate. A retry after a deadlock or serialisation failure must rerun the reads as well as the writes, because the facts on which it decides may have changed. Repeating arbitrary external effects inside that loop can charge or notify someone twice.</p>
<p>The database transaction also ends before an email provider delivers Mina’s confirmation. To avoid losing that work when the application crashes after commit, store a notification job in the same transaction as the order. A background worker reads the job and sends the email later. This <strong>outbox</strong> makes the intent durable; it does not make the provider call part of the database transaction. A worker can lose the provider’s reply too, so safe repetition or reconciliation is still needed at that boundary.</p>
<p>The application boundary matters on a read too. The history query used Mina’s customer number, 1. Letting a caller substitute 2 would return Tariq’s orders just as correctly. The application has to establish who is asking and check which orders they may read. It must also keep supplied values separate from SQL instructions. With <strong>query parameters</strong>, it sends those values separately from the statement text, so a value cannot become part of its SQL syntax.</p>
<p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Retries when a commit reply is missing</a> follows one identified purchase through uncertainty. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/outboxes-and-jobs/">Outboxes and database-backed jobs</a> follows the external work, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/application-sql/">SQL at the application boundary</a> follows a request through parameter binding and the connection it uses. Later, when the shop changes its tables, old and new application versions may run together. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/schema-changes/">Live schema changes and backfills</a> develops a change that lets both keep working during that transition.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="copies"><h2 id="copies">Copies and partitions change where a request goes</h2>
<p>A single database server can be the shared authority for many application instances. Adding another database server can solve a different problem: keep service available after a machine fails, move reads nearer to readers, or share a workload that no longer fits one machine. The arrangement determines which of those it actually achieves.</p>
<p>A <strong>replica</strong> keeps another copy of the same data. In a common arrangement, one primary accepts changes and sends them to replicas. A replica that applies them later can answer a history query before order 103 has arrived, even after Mina received success from the primary. Reading her next page from the primary, or waiting for a replica to reach a known committed point, avoids that stale follow-up. A general “send reads to replicas” rule does not.</p>
<p>Making commit wait for another machine to store the change can protect against losing the first machine’s disk. It also adds communication to the commit path and can hold up writes when the required copy is unavailable. “Synchronous” needs a precise meaning: receiving log data, storing it durably and replaying it so queries can see the order are different milestones.</p>
<p><strong>Partitioning</strong> divides data into pieces. When those pieces live on independently serving database machines, the arrangement is often called <strong>sharding</strong>. For example, different shops might have their records on different shards. A request needs a routing rule to find its shard; a report over all shops has to gather results from several. Splitting one popular product’s buyers across machines does not remove the need to agree who gets its last mug.</p>
<p>An application can manage separate shards itself, keeping most transactions inside one. A distributed relational system can instead coordinate a transaction across partitions while presenting a SQL interface. That coordination preserves an all-or-nothing decision spanning servers, but has to exchange messages and cope with machines failing independently. The relevant latency comes from the participating data and copies, not just the nearest SQL endpoint.</p>
<p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-read-routing/">Replicas, lag and read routing</a> follows the stale read. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/partitioning-and-sharding/">Sharding and distributed SQL</a> compares ways to divide the work, and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/distributed-transactions/">Transactions across partitions</a> explains how a decision survives a participating server’s failure.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="recovery"><h2 id="recovery">Recovery starts with what was lost</h2>
<p>A database that holds orders needs a named owner and a plan for restoring service. The first distinction is what failed. Three incidents can all look like “the database is down” while needing different recovery paths.</p>
<div class="table-scroll intro-table" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Three recovery paths and what each preserves"><table><caption>Choose the recovery path from the incident</caption><thead><tr><th>Incident</th><th>Recovery path</th><th>Question to answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Server process crashes; its storage survives.</td><td><strong>Crash recovery</strong>: restart using files and log.</td><td>Can it recover the acknowledged orders, and how long does restart take?</td></tr>
<tr><td>The writer’s machine cannot serve requests.</td><td><strong>Failover</strong>: transfer write authority to a suitable replica.</td><td>Which commits reached it, and how is the old writer prevented from accepting more?</td></tr>
<tr><td>A mistaken deletion committed and reached the replicas.</td><td><strong>Restore</strong>: recover a retained earlier copy, possibly replaying saved log to a chosen point.</td><td>Which later valid orders must be reconciled after returning to that point?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Crash recovery preserves committed mistakes. Replication copies them. A backup retained separately from the live service gives recovery a history that the mistake has not overwritten. Restoring to before a deletion can also remove valid purchases committed afterwards, so recovery includes comparing and reconciling those purchases with the world outside the database.</p>
<p>Two objectives make the plan testable. The <strong>recovery point objective</strong> is how much recent data loss the service can accept; the <strong>recovery time objective</strong> is how long it can take to restore service. Values such as “at most five minutes of data” and “back within an hour” are requirements to test, not properties obtained merely by enabling backups. Include finding the incident, obtaining credentials, restoring the data, validating orders and reconnecting the application in the rehearsal.</p>
<p>The owner also needs bounded access, storage headroom and observations that lead to action: a growing connection queue, blocked purchases, failing backups or a replica falling behind. A managed service can perform much of the machinery, while the application team still chooses the objectives, access policy and acceptable result. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backup, restore and reconciliation</a>, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/failover/">Failover and fencing</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/access-control/">Database access and least privilege</a> supply the working detail.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="choice"><h2 id="choice">Choose an arrangement from the work it must support</h2>
<p>The relational model does not require a separate server or a cluster. An embedded engine runs inside the application process; a conventional database server accepts connections from other processes; a distributed relational system coordinates data across several servers. All can expose tables and SQL. The difference is where authority, resources and failure boundaries sit.</p>
<div class="table-scroll intro-table" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="First shortlist of relational arrangements"><table><caption>A first shortlist, before a representative trial</caption><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Arrangement worth trying</th><th>Evidence that may change the choice</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A device, desktop tool or service whose data and writes stay local.</td><td><strong>Embedded</strong>, such as SQLite. No separate database service to operate.</td><td>Many independent writers need to share authority across machines. SQLite allows only one writer at a time per database file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A shared application with many concurrent requests and short transactions.</td><td><strong>Conventional server</strong>, such as PostgreSQL, MySQL with InnoDB, or SQL Server, often managed.</td><td>A measured capacity or placement requirement remains after query, index and contention repairs; the required failure survival needs a different topology.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Writes and data must be distributed, with transactions spanning their placement.</td><td><strong>Distributed relational</strong>, such as CockroachDB, with deliberate replica and region placement.</td><td>The real transaction’s coordination latency, retry rate, feature compatibility and cost under the failures it must survive.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For this shared shop, a managed conventional server is a defensible first candidate if it meets the workload and recovery requirements. For an offline till holding its own local records, an embedded engine may be a better starting point. A managed distributed database becomes worth its coordination cost when the placement, write scale or failure requirement earns it. These are starting hypotheses; a large table alone does not establish the need for more servers.</p>
<p>Reports change the work. The purchase finds a few rows and changes them quickly, a <strong>transactional</strong> workload. A sales report may scan years of order lines, read only a few columns, and group millions of values, an <strong>analytical</strong> workload. A column-oriented relational engine can pack and process those columns together; DuckDB is an embedded example for analytical work. “Embedded” therefore says where an engine runs, not whether it suits purchases or reports.</p>
<p>A conventional server can handle both kinds of query while the volume and concurrency fit. As reporting grows, it can compete with purchases for memory, CPU and storage bandwidth. A replica may separate some read load but can be stale and retains its engine’s storage design. A separate analytical copy gives reports a different engine and resource budget, at the cost of moving changes, monitoring delay and deciding how corrections propagate. <a href="/learn/databases/column-stores/">Column stores</a> explains when reading by column changes the work.</p>
<p>Before choosing, write down the important operations and the promises they need: how many purchases arrive together, how many touch P7, what the largest report reads, where callers are, which failures must be survived, how fresh a following read must be, and how quickly recovery must finish. Try those operations on data with a realistic size and distribution. Check the answers and concurrent outcomes as well as latency, resource use, restore time and cost. A benchmark of unrelated lookups cannot settle whether this shop can sell its last mug correctly.</p>
<p>To begin using a database, start with <a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relations, keys and normalisation</a>, then <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Joins and the meaning of a result row</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/transaction-boundaries/">Transactions and isolation</a>. To make an existing service faster, follow <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">Query execution and bad plans</a>. To take responsibility for its survival, begin with <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backup, restore and reconciliation</a>. The later implementation branches let you inspect these mechanisms in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/">PostgreSQL</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/keys-and-ranges/">CockroachDB</a>.</p>
</section>

